#
# crypttab keyscript: unlock root the usual way, and leave the passphrase where
# the first-boot wizard can find it.
#
# The wizard grows the LUKS mapping into the rest of the drive, and rebuilding a
# dm-crypt mapping needs the volume key. That key cannot be recovered after boot:
# cryptsetup hands it to the kernel as a "logon" key, whose payload userspace is
# never permitted to read back, and the keyring holding it belongs to the
# initramfs process that unlocked the disk, which is gone once init takes over.
# The dm table shows only a dangling reference to it. So the passphrase has to be
# kept here, at the one moment it exists in the clear, or the wizard has to ask
# for it a second time a few minutes after boot.
#
# It goes to /run, which is tmpfs and is moved onto the real root at pivot, so it
# reaches the wizard without ever being written to the stick. The wizard deletes
# it the moment it is used, and drops keyscript= from crypttab when setup
# finishes, so a settled stick neither stashes nor holds anything.
#
# cryptroot runs this as `run_keyscript | unlock_mapping`, which makes stdout the
# key material itself. Nothing but the passphrase may be printed; everything else
# goes to stderr.

STASH_DIR=/run/portlin
STASH="$STASH_DIR/luks-pass"

# Deliberately no `set -e`. Every failure below is a failure to make the wizard's
# life easier, and none of them is worth refusing to boot the machine over.

pass=$(/lib/cryptsetup/askpass "Unlock $CRYPTTAB_NAME (passphrase): ")

# Best effort, and silent: a full or missing /run costs the wizard one extra
# prompt later, which is not worth a message on a console that is showing a
# passphrase prompt. The umask covers the directory and the file in one go and
# leaves no moment where either exists at the default mode; mkdir -m would not,
# since with -p it applies to the deepest directory only and says nothing about
# the file. Root-only is as private as this can be, and root already holds the
# volume key the passphrase protects.
(
    umask 077
    mkdir -p "$STASH_DIR" && printf '%s' "$pass" > "$STASH"
) 2>/dev/null || true

printf '%s' "$pass"
